V}$ vs. silver/silver chloride $\mathrm{(Ag/AgCl)}$ , respectively. This work provides a novel strategy for investigating the applications of promising two-dimensional semiconductive MOF as multifunctional electrocatalysts with boosted electrocatalytic
activities in energy fields.
## INTRODUCTION
Decreasing fossil fuel supplies and increasing energy demands and environmental issues have triggered tremendous efforts toward the development of new clean energy sources and energy conversion methods [1]. $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is considered to be an ideal energy carrier for future applications because it is a renewable and zero-emission energy source in contrast to current fossil fuels (coal, oil, and natural gas) [2,3]. The whole electrocatalytic water splitting process is composed of two half reactions: the anodic oxygen evolution reaction (OER) and the cathodic hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) [4]. This process has great potential application in sustainable $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production due to its large scale of raw materials and capability to be generated by using sun and wind, which are all renewable energy sources [5]. Another technology, direct methanol fuel cell (DMFC), is considered to be promising energy sources owing to its advantages of high conversion efficiency, high energy density, potentially renewable features, and simple system design [6,7]. Methanol oxidation reaction (MOR) is the key electrode reaction determining DMFC conversion efficiency [8]. Pt group metals are considered as the most effective catalysts for HER and MOR, whereas Ir/Ru-based alloys are regarded as superior OER catalysts [9,10]. However, the high cost and scarcity of rare metal-based catalysts with excellent catalytic performances greatly limit their wide-ranging applications. Highly effective OER electrocatalysts are key factors because the OER is the bottleneck of water splitting, and poisoning effects hinder the development of Ptbased catalysts for the MOR [11,12]. Therefore, developing inexpensive and earth abundant electrocatalysts that concurrently exhibit high efficiency toward HER, OER, and MOR is urgent.
Although many efforts have been devoted to the development of electrocatalysts based on transition metal phosphides, sulfides, and chalcogenides [13], most of these electrocatalysts only exhibit sole HER, OER, or MOR performance. Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), which are a class of hybrid organic–inorganic porous materials consisting of inorganic secondary building units (metal ions or clusters) linked to organic bridging ligands via coordination bonds, have aroused the growing attention of researchers and have been explored in various fields, such as gas absorption and separation, catalysis, sensors, advanced therapies, and energy storage [14] owing to their own unique structures, including high surface areas, porosity, tunable pore structures, and chemical tunability [15-17]. MOF-based and MOF-derived electrocatalysts have been extensively constructed and applied for overall water splitting [18], zinc–air batteries [19], $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction [20], CO oxidation [21], and the MOR [22]. However, due to the poor electrochemical activity of the pristine bulk MOF [23], MOF-based nanomaterials are usually treated through various approaches, such as pyrolysis at high temperatures [24], sulfurization [25], phosphation [26], and integration with conductive additives (carbon nanomaterials [27], inorganic nanomaterials [28], and noble metals [29]). These complicated strategies greatly increase the difficulty of controlling MOF structure and the cost. Therefore, MOFbased nanomaterials are treated through a feasible method is highly desirable.
Recently, $\pi$ -conjugated molecules were employed to construct planar conductive
MOF owing to their high charge mobility [30,31]. In contrast to traditional conductive materials, such as carbon nanotubes, graphene, and polyaniline, conductive MOFs possess a crystalline state, tunable band gap, and a designable charge transport pathway [32,33]. Two-dimensional (2D) semiconductive MOFs have demonstrated great importance in electrocatalysis because they exhibit atomic ordering that is mediated by electronic communication through metal nodes, planar MOFs along 2D directions with fully in-plane $\pi$ -delocalization, and weak out-plane $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking [34-36]. Metallic nodes and organic linkers within 2D conductive MOFs can serve as the source of charge carriers, which are beneficial for improving electrocatalytic performances [37]. Most of the reported 2D conductive or semiconductive MOFs display stacked honeycomb lattices and are composed of a variety of late-transition-metal ions (e.g., Cu, Ni, Co, or Pt) and benzene- or triphenylene-derived ligands with ortho-disubstituted N, O, or S donor atoms, such as benzene hexathiol (BHT) [38], 1,2,5,6,9,10-triphenylenehexathiol [39], 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexaiminotriphenylene (HITP) [40], 2,3,6,7,10,11- hexahydroxytriphenylene (HHTP) [41], and hexaaminobenzene (HAB) [42]. Graphene-like Cu–BHT with different morphologies displayed high conductivity and was utilized as an outstanding electrocatalyst for HER [43]. A conductive 2D $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ MOF used as an superior oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) electrocatalyst showed an onset potential of 0.82 V toward ORR [40]. Conductive few-layer $[\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}]_{\mathrm{n}}$ MOF nanosheets fabricated layer-by-layer demonstrated the 2D $\pi$ - conjugated structure, which greatly enhanced the conductivity and remarkably improved maximize the catalytic performance toward OER [44]. However, rare report
on conductive or semiconductive MOFs as multifunctional electrocatalysts for overall water splitting and MOR has been found. Since small bridging ligands demonstrate enhanced metallic behaviors due to the short distance and good overlap between the electronic wave functions of adjacent ligands and metals [42], conductive or semiconductive metallic HAB MOFs have the promising applications as comprehensive and multiple functional electrocatalysts for MOR and overall water splitting.   
Given that HAB is a highly delocalized and conjugated organic molecule that has an excellent coordination structure with metal cations, MOFs prepared using HAB as ligand have considerable application potential in the fields of clean energy and energy conversion [45]. In this work, a series of 2D semiconductive bimetallic $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOFs have been synthesized, which were proved to be consisted of redox-active HAB and $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Ni}$ ion nodes. The nanostructures and chemical components of bimetallic $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}M O F}$ s were modulated by changing the ratios of Co and Ni precursors. Given the synergistic effect that occurred in the combination of the different metal atoms of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF, the catalytic performances of the obtained catalysts toward non-Pt MOR and overall water splitting were superior to those of sole metalbased MOF catalysts $[\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ and
application as (b) a bifunctional electrocatalyst for MOR and overall water splitting.
$\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOF]. The electrochemical measurements revealed that $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 with a mass ratio of Co:Ni of 1:3 displayed a high current density of $92.8~\mathrm{mA}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at 1.6 V vs. reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) toward MOR and low overpotentials of $119\mathrm{mV}$ and $1.35\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE at the current density of $10\mathrm{mAcm^{-1}}$ for the HER and OER, respectively. The voltages applied to the three-electrode and twoelectrode system based on $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 used for water splitting were 1.52 V vs. RHE and $1.45\mathrm{~V~}$ , respectively. The present strategy for developing robust multifunctional electrocatalysts based on the 2D semiconducting $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}M O F}$ illustrated three innovations: (i) the coexistence of bimetal ions in metallic clusters can enrich the structural diversities of MOF materials and improve their electrocatalytic activity [46]; (ii) the unique $\pi$ –d conjugated conductive network accounted for the high electrical conductivity of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOFs [47]; and (iii) multiple catalytic performances for the MOR and water splitting in alkaline solution were observed. The present work provides a novel strategy for investigating the applications of promising semiconductive and 2D MOFs as multifunctional electrocatalysts with boosted electrocatalytic activities in energy fields.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
The contents about materials and regents, basic characterizations, and electrochemical measurements were provided in the S1 section (See the Supporting Information).
Synthesis of Series of the $\mathbf{C_{0_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Ni_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}}}$ MOFs. The series of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOFs were synthesized using a modified method according to the reported literature [48]. Briefly, taking the preparation of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-1 as an instance, $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O\ (20\ m g)}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(20~\mathrm{mg})$ with a mass ratio of 1:1 were dissolved in $5\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water containing polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP, $5\mathrm{mg}$ ) by continuous stirring for $15~\mathrm{min}$ to obtain a homogeneous solution, followed by adding ammonium hydroxide ( $120\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , 6 M). After that, the 1-methyl-2-pyrrolidinone solution of HAB ( $4~\mathrm{mL}$ , $5~\mathrm{{mg}~\mathrm{{mL^{-1}}}}$ ) was added into the above mixture and was maintained at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ without stirring. Then, the above solution was transferred to a $100~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave and heated at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{7\mathrm{h}}$ . After cooling to the room temperature, the resulting precipitate was collected by centrifuging, following by washing with deionized water for three times. The obtained solid powder was dried overnight under $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in vacuum oven, and denoted as $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-1. The $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}M O F}$ at other mass ratios $\mathrm{Co}{:}\mathrm{Ni}=1{:}3$ and 1:5) were also synthesized using the same method, and denoted as $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 and $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-3, separately. For comparison, the sole metal MOFs, $\mathrm{Co_{3}(H A B)_{2}\ M O]}$ F and $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOF, were synthesized using same procedure without adding $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ into the preparation system, separately.
Electrochemical MOR Measurements. Cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements were carried out for evaluating the MOR activities of the series of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOFs with the potential range from $-0.2\mathrm{V}$ to $0.6\mathrm{V}$ vs. $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ at a scan rate of 50 $\mathrm{mV~s^{-1}}$ , for which 1.0 M KOH containing 0.5 M $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ as the electrolyte. In prior to each measurement, the solution was purged with $\Nu_{2}$ $(99.99~\%)$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ to remove dissolved oxygen.
Electrochemical Measurements for HER, OER, and Overall Water Splitting.
The OER performances of all catalysts were evaluated by linear sweep voltammetry (LSV), which was recorded in an $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -saturated 1.0 M KOH at a scan rate of $10\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ using a rotating disk electrode (RDE) at a rotation speed of $1600~\mathrm{rpm}$ . The HER performances of all samples were also recorded by LSV curves with the potential range from $-1.0\mathrm{~V~}$ to -1.6 V vs. Ag/AgCl. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were performed at a constant potential which current density achieved $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ within the frequency from $0.1\mathrm{Hz}$ to ${10^{6}\mathrm{Hz}}$ . The electrochemical doublelayer capacitance $(\mathbf{C}_{d l})$ was determined using a simple CV method. The scan rate was measured from 5 to $55\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ with a scan rare of ${5\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}}$ , whereas the voltage window was ranging from 0.05 to $0.15{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. Ag/AgCl. As such, the slope was the $\mathrm{C}_{d l}$ value, which was calculated from linear fitting. Additionally, the stability tests were performed by chronoamperometry (CA) at a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . The overall water splitting test was concurrently carried out in both three-electrode and two-electrode systems, while the same catalyst was simultaneously used as the anode and cathode in the two-electrode system. For comparison, the commercial $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}(20\ \%)$ and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ catalysts were respectively used in the HER and OER measurements. Moreover, the amount of the produced gases $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)$ of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 catalyst during electrolyzer test were collected, while the H-type cell equipped with an isolation membrane was applied to avoid gas mixing. Furthermore, the turnover number (TON) and turnover frequency (TOF) were calculated by using the following equations [49]:
$$
\begin{array}{r}{T O N=\frac{m o l e s o f e v o l v e d H2}{m o l e s o f c a t a l y s t s}}\end{array}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{r}{T O F=\frac{T O N}{r e a c t i o n t i m e}}\end{array}
$$
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSIONS
Surface Morphologies and Nanostructures of the Series of $\mathbf{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)}_{2}$ MOFs. The surface morphologies and microstructures of the series of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOFs were investigated by Field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM) and Transmission electron microscope (TEM) techniques. The details about the nanosheet structure of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOF are supplied in the S2 section (See the Supporting Information). The results
reveal no substantial difference among the series of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOFs and the sole metallic MOFs is observed. As indicated in Figs. S1, S2, and S3, the series of semiconductive $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOFs are composed of a large number of nanoparticles with uniform sizes that have accumulated into large blocks. However, their sizes of the nanoparticles and blocks are quite different. As demonstrated in Fig. S3, the nanoparticle and block sizes of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-3 are larger than those of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-1 and $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2, suggesting that MOF nanoparticles are apt to agglomerate when excessive metal ions were used in the preparation procedure of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOFs. The high contents of amorphous structures in the $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF prepared using large amounts of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions primarily account for this tendency, which is proven by the amorphous structure of the $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOF (S2, See the Supporting Information).   
The specific surface areas of the series of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOFs were investigated by the $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms at $77\mathrm{K}$ . As shown in Fig. S5a, all the samples show a type II adsorption profile. The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface areas, pore volume and total pore volume of these materials are displayed in Table S1. Clearly, such a 2D layered structure endows $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-1 with a much higher specific surface area $(890.3\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ) than those of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 $(784.2\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-3 $(725.6\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ). Large surface area provides more active sites, which are beneficial to the catalytic performance enhancement [50]. And all three samples display a narrow distribution centered at $0.5{-}1~\mathrm{nm}$ and a wide distribution around $2\mathrm{-}8\mathrm{nm}$ in the mesopore range (Fig. S2b).
