As displayed in Fig. 1, the TEM images of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-1 (Figs. 1a and b) reveal a nanosheet-like structure, which is similar to the structure of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOF. The clear lattice spacing of $0.21\ \mathrm{nm}$ found in the HR-TEM image of $\mathrm{Co}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Ni}_{3}$ - $\mathbf{\Sigma}_{\mathrm{x}}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOF-1 (Fig. 1c) is assigned to the (111) lattice plane of the CoNi alloy [46]. The thick black regions in the TEM images of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 and $\mathrm{Co}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Ni}_{3}$ - $\mathbf{\Sigma}_{\mathrm{x}}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOF-3 (Figs. 1d, e, g, and h) indicate that these MOFs nanosheets are thicker than those of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-1. Also, the same lattice fringe of $0.21\ \mathrm{nm}$ attributed to the (111) plane of the CoNi alloy is observed in the HR-TEM images of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 and $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-3 (Figs. 1f and i). These findings reveal that the 2D nanosheet structure of bimetallic $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF is remarkably affected by the dosage of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions, which increases the amorphous structures of the semiconductive MOFs. Unique 2D and amorphous structures can also increase active sites and defects, which play a crucial role in enhancing electrocatalytic activities toward the OER [51,52]. Moreover, a synergistic effect occurs by combining Co and Ni ions, further boosting catalytic performances toward the MOR, HER, and OER [53,54]. The SEM and energy dispersive spectrometer mapping images indicate that Co, Ni, C, O and N elements are uniformly distributed within the MOF nanosheets of the series of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOFs (Fig. S4)
Crystal and Chemical Structures of the Series of $\mathbf{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)}_{2}$ MOFs.
Different techniques, such as powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD), Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy, Raman spectroscopy, and X-ray photoelectron spectrometry (XPS) were carried out for further probing the crystal and chemical structures of the series of semiconductive MOFs. The PXRD pattern of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOF (curve i, Fig. S6a) shows three main peaks at $10.97^{\circ}$ , $16.04^{\circ}$ , and $22.16^{\circ}$ that are consistent with previously reported peaks [48]. The peak located at $34.0^{\circ}$ is assigned to the (111) plane of CoO (JCPDS card no. 42-1300) [55]. Two distinctive diffraction peaks are determined at $15.29^{\circ}$ and $27.69^{\circ}$ for the $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOF (curve ii, Fig. S6a) and were similar to those reported in the literature [42]. The same peak position is achieved for the series of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOFs (curve iii- $\cdot\nu$ , Fig. S6a), hinting that $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions more easily coordinate with HAB to form an amorphous structure, which play a dominant role as compared with $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ ions. Additionally, the FT-IR spectra of the series of MOFs (Fig. S6b) exhibit similar characteristic peaks. The broad absorption peaks at $3200{-}3400~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be attributed to N–H stretching, whereas the peaks at 1646, 1444, and $1117~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are assigned to N–H bending, $C{=}C$ stretching, and C–N stretching, respectively [42]. This finding indicates that the series of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOFs are composed of metal ions and diverse N-related groups. As displayed in Fig. S6c, the Raman spectra of the series of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOFs exhibit a main peak at 1523 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which is attributed to symmetric ring stretching. Consistent with the XRD results, the Raman spectra of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-1, $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2, and $\mathrm{Co}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Ni}_{3}$ - $_\mathrm{x}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}\mathrm{MOF}{-}3$ display the same peak positions as the Raman spectra of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}\mathrm{MOF}$ . The chemical structures and components of the series of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOFs were also analyzed by using XPS characterizations (Fig. S6d), wherein the signals of Co $2p$ (778 eV), Ni $2p$ (853 eV), C 1s (284.6 eV), N 1s (400.1 eV), and O $1s(530\mathrm{eV})$ coexist in the XPS survey scan spectra of MOFs. The atomic $\%$ of each element of all semiconductive MOFs is summarized in Table S2. It demonstrates that the ratios of Ni/Co of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-1, $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2, and $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-3 are 1.03, 1.67, and 2.61, respectively, which are close to the experimental values. Notably, the O 1s signal with high content is observed in the series of MOFs, owing that the samples were contaminated by $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ when determined by XPS [56]. Highresolution XPS spectra of each element containing in all samples were analyzed by using XPSPEAK1 software for investigating the chemical valence and environment of each element. The Co $2p$ , and Ni $2p$ XPS spectra of the series of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOFs are illustrated in Fig. 2, whereas the C 1s, N 1s, and O 1s XPS spectra are provided in Fig. S9.
