CV curves were recorded in $1.0\mathbf{M}$ KOH containing methanol with concentrations ranging from $0.1\mathbf{M}$ to $0.7{\bf M}$ at a scan rate of $50\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ for investigating the influence of methanol concentration on the electrocatalytic activity of the $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}M O F}.$ 2 catalyst (Fig. 3d). The result demonstrates that the peak current density recorded at 1.6 V vs. RHE increases with increasing the methanol concentration. The result implies that the peak current density of the electrode is proportional to the methanol concentration (inset of Fig. 3d). Furthermore, the CV curve of the $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 catalyst was recorded in $1.0\mathrm{M}$ KOH containing $0.5{\mathrm{~M~}}$ methanol at different scan rates ranging from $50\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ to $400\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ , as displayed in Fig. 3e. It shows that the CV curve shape of the electrode does not substantially change, whereas the enclosed area increases with increasing the scan rate due to fast reaction kinetics. A linear relationship is observed between the oxidation peak current density and the square root of scan rate (inset of Fig. 3e), revealing that the electrocatalytic reaction is controlled by a diffusion-controlled process.   
The electron transfer mechanism of the $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF catalyst surface in
the MOR in alkaline media possibly obeys the following steps:
$$
\mathrm{NiOOH+CH_{3}O H+1.25O_{2}\longrightarrow N i(O H)_{2}+C O_{2}+1.5H_{2}O},
$$
First, when the as-prepared catalyst was immersed in $1.0\mathbf{M}$ KOH, $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ ions play an important role in MOR [60], leading to the formation of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ (and/or Co[OH]2). Afterward, $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ (and/or ${\mathrm{Co}}[{\mathrm{OH}}]_{2}$ ) was oxidized into NiOOH (or CoOOH), which is a strong oxidant in the anodic direction [61]. Then, they acted as the electron transfer mediator in MOR process on the electrode surfaces, while partial NiOOH (or CoOOH) components were reduced to $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ (and/or ${\mathrm{Co}}[{\mathrm{OH}}]_{2}$ ) simultaneously. This reaction greatly reduces the amount of NiOOH (or CoOOH) on the electrode surface [58,62]. Furthermore, large amounts of NiOOH species were reduced to $\mathrm{Ni(OH)}_{2}$ when the methanol concentration was high, thus resulting in the high oxidation current density of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ relative to that of NiOOH. In addition, the reduction peak current decreases with increasing the methanol concentration likely because of the consumption of NiOOH [60,63]. Then, the residual amount of NiOOH on the catalyst surface decreases with increasing the methanol concentration, as illustrated in Fig. 3c.
Fig. 3f shows the charonoamperogram (CA) curves of the five catalysts at the oxidation peak potential of $1.6\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE acquired in $1.0\mathrm{M}$ KOH containing $0.5{\mathrm{~M~}}$ methanol for 3000 s. Only a slight change in the current densities of the five catalysts is observed at the beginning. During the test procedure, however, bubbles were formed, leading to a negative effect on CA curves. All results reveal that the $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 catalyst displays outstandingly electrocatalytic activity toward MOR and good stability, which may be primarily owing to the following factors: (i) electron transfer is greatly improved by the mixed metal valence states of $\mathrm{Co^{0}}/\mathrm{Co^{2+}}/\mathrm{Co^{3+}}$ and $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}/N i^{3+}}.$ ， graphene-like nanostructure, and large specific surface area of the porous $\mathrm{Co}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Ni}_{3}$ - $\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathrm{x}}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOF, thus enhancing catalytic ability; (ii) Co–N and Ni–N coordination centers display excellent intrinsic catalytic activities toward the MOR [64]; and (iii) 2D nanosheets and amorphous structures furnish additional active sites and defects, providing a convenient environment for electron transfer between active sites and electrode surfaces, thus further improving electrocatalytic MOR performance [13]. Additionally, the electrochemical activities of all MOFs were characterized by EIS technique. Fig. ${\mathbf{39}}$ shows the EIS Nyquist plots of the series of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ $\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOFs, which were simulated by using an equivalent circuit (inset of Fig. $\mathbf{3g}$ ). The electron transfer resistance values $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ of the five electrodes between the electrode surface and electrolyte were deduced, as summarized in Table S3. It shows that the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOF electrode provides a small $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ value $(40~\Omega)$ than that of the $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOF $(52.4\ \Omega)$ , suggesting its superior electrochemical activity. Among the series of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOFs, the $\mathrm{Co}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Ni}_{3}$ - $\mathbf{\Sigma}_{\mathrm{x}}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOF-2 electrode has the smallest $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ value $(18.2\Omega)$ , hinting at fast electron transfer on the electrode surface. As compared, the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOFs exhibit inferior electrochemical performances owing to their relatively large $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ values.
All these results hint that integrating $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ coordination centers with nitrogen atoms bearing on HAB can remarkably improve the electrochemical activity of bimetallic $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}M O F}$ .
## Electrocatalytic Performances of the Series of $\mathbf{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)}_{2}$ MOFs toward
HER. The HER performances of the series of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOFs in 1.0 M KOH were evaluated by acquiring LSV curves with a scan rate of $5~\mathrm{mV~s^{-1}}$ (Fig. 4a). The LSV curves of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOF, $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOF, and $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}(20\%)$ were also obtained for comparison (Fig. S10a). The two parameters of onset potential $(\upeta_{0})$ and overpotential $(\upeta_{10})$ are defined as the potential needed to acquire the current densities of 1 and $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively, and were used to assess the catalytic efficiency of the as-prepared catalysts [65]. The $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{n}}_{0}$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{n}}_{10}$ values of all catalysts are summarized in Table S5. The results show that the $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{n}}_{10}$ required by the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOF catalyst is 202 mV, which is higher than that required by the $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOF ( $\mathrm{186~mV)}$ , suggesting its inferior HER activity. As expected, the commercial $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}(20\%)$ requires low $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{n}}_{0}$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{n}}_{10}$ values of 10 and $76~\mathrm{mV}$ , respectively, indicating its outstanding electrochemical activity toward HER. The series of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOFs provide considerably reduced onset potentials and overpotentials, hinting their good HER performances. Among the three $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF, the $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 has the smallest η10 of $119~\mathrm{mV}$ . The catalytic activity of the $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 catalyst is comparable with or superior to those of other reported $\mathrm{Co-}$ or Ni-based and bulk MOFbased HER electrocatalysts (Fig. 4c and Table S6).
