The total reaction has been well documented to involve three possible elementary steps as shown below:
(1) The electrochemical adsorption of hydrogen onto the active site (Volmer step)
followed by either (2) electrochemical desorption (Heyrovsky step)
$\mathrm{~\boldmath~\Omega~}_{1}\mathrm{~\boldmath~\Omega~}\ast\mathrm{~\boldmath~-~}H_{a d s}+H_{2}O\mathrm{~\boldmath~+~}e^{-}\leftrightarrow\ast\mathrm{~\boldmath~+~}H_{2}\mathrm{~+~}O H^{-}\ ,$ (10) or (3) chemical desorption (Tafel step)
$$
2*\mathrm{~-~}H_{a d s}\leftrightarrow2*\mathrm{~+~}H_{2}.
$$
Durability and long-term stability are two crucial parameters for evaluating the catalytic performance of an electrocatalyst toward HER in alkaline solution. Herein, the chronoamperpmetric curve was tested to probe the durability of the developed $\mathrm{Co}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Ni}_{3}.$ - $\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathrm{x}}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOF-2 catalyst at a constant voltage of $-0.12\mathrm{~V~}$ (Fig. 4d). The curve shows that current density of the $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 catalyst retains $96.7\%$ of its initial value after testing for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ . By contrast, the current density of the commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}(20\ \%)$ catalyst is only $71.6\%$ of its initial value under the same conditions. Furthermore, the stability of the $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}\ M O F{-}{2}}$ catalyst was determined by LSV curves under the same conditions. As shown in Fig. 4e, no substantial change is observed between the 5000th LSV cycle of the $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 catalyst and the first cycle, along with the negligible loss of the current density This result reveals the outstanding stability of the $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 catalyst in HER in alkaline solution.
OER Performance of the Series of $\mathbf{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOFs in Alkaline Solution.
The electrocatalytic OER performances of the series of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOFs, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOF, $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOF, and commercial ${\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}$ were investigated in $1.0\mathrm{M}$ KOH by LSV with a scan rate of $10~\mathrm{mV~s^{-1}}$ (Figs. 5 and S11). A low onset potential, low overpotential, and high current density represent the excellent OER performance of catalysts [66]. As shown in Fig. S11a, the $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{n}}_{0}$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{n}}_{10}$ of the $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}\mathrm{MOF}$ are 1.27 and $1.44\mathrm{V}$ , respectively, which are slightly lower than those of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOF $(\boldsymbol{\eta}_{0}=1.32\:\mathrm{V}$ , $\eta_{10}=1.50\mathrm{V},$ ), indicating the superior OER performance of this MOF. Fig. 5a indicates that the OER performances of the series of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}M O F}$ catalysts are superior to that of the $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ catalyst. Notably, the $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-1 and $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 display the same $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{n}}_{0}$ of $1.35{\mathrm{~V~}}$ . These results verify that the synergistic effect of Co and Ni ions can effectively reduce the onset potential toward OER. However, the $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 catalyst exhibits the lowest $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{n}}_{10}$ of $1.40\mathrm{V}$ among all samples, hinting at its outstanding catalytic activity toward OER. Additionally, the electrocatalytic OER performance of the $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 is better than that of other reported OER catalysts (Fig. 5c and Table S8), such as NiCo– POM/Ni (1.58 V) [32], Co–Mo–S/CC (1.53 V) [67], Ni–Mo (1.53 V) [68], and Co4Mo2@NC (1.56 V) [69]. The Tafel plots of the five kinds of MOFs and commercial ${\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}$ were calculated from their LSV curves (Fig. 5b). The Tafel slope of the $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 catalyst is as small as $26~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}}$ , which is smaller than those of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-1 $(28\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ ), CoxNi3-x(HAB)2 MOF-3 $(92\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec^{-1}}$ ), and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}(52\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1})$ (Table S7), hinting its faster OER kinetics . The results also suggest the OER performance of the $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2is superior other prepared catalysts and other Co- or Ni-related catalysts (Table S8). All of the results reveal that the asprepared $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 catalysts show excellent catalytic activity toward OER because of the following reasons: (i) the high electrical conductivity caused by the strong $\pi{-}\mathrm{d}$ conjunction can remarkably facilitate charge transport during
electrocatalytic transformations, resulting in low overpotentials for the OER [70]; (ii) the structural superiority of 2D ultrathin nanosheets and a large electrochemical surface area boost rapid diffusion throughout electrocatalysts, leading to an enhancement in active site densities [71]; and (iii) the partial electron transfer from $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ sites to $\mathrm{Co}^{3+}$ sites greatly improves the OER performance of bimetallic MOFs [72].   
Chronoamperometry was performed at a constant potential of 1.40 V vs. RHE to achieve the current density of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ to probe the long-term stability of the $\mathrm{Co}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Ni}_{3}$ - $\mathbf{\Sigma}_{\mathrm{x}}(\mathrm{HAB})_{2}$ MOF-2 catalyst (Fig. 5d). The current density has a slightly decrease of $3.6\%$ over $48\mathrm{~h~}$ in comparison with its initial value. However, the current density of the commercial ${\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}$ catalyst exhibits a remarkable decrease of $22.8\%$ after $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ Additionally, the LSV curve of the $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}M O F{-}{2}}$ catalyst only demonstrates a negligible change after 5000 cycles (Fig. 5e). All results reveal the good stability of the developed catalyst over the long-term test. Thus, the catalyst is a promising OER catalyst for alleviating the dependence on noble-metal-based catalysts.   
