Basic characteristics were investigated by XPS, XRD, SEM, and element mapping to assess the stability of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 after use. High-resolution $\mathrm{Co}~2p$ and Ni $2p$ XPS spectra are shown in Figs. S13a and b. The peak positions of $\mathrm{Co}2p$ and Ni $2p$ are unchanged, whereas the peak intensity of Co $2p$ decreases. This result suggests that a part of Co ions have dissolved into the electrolyte during the measurement. The characteristic peaks in the XRD pattern of the used $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 catalyst is unchanged, along with the comparable peak intensity with the fresh one (Fig. S13c). As displayed in the FE-SEM image of the used catalyst (Fig. S13d), no evident variation in surface morphology has occurred. Also, Co and Ni are homogeneously distributed in the used catalyst (Fig. S13e). The above results show that the as-prepared $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 catalyst can maintain long-term stability in alkaline solution.
## CONCLUSION
Novel non-Pt and bifunctional electrocatalysts based on 2D bimetallic semiconductive $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF was developed for the MOR and overall water splitting in an alkaline medium. Their electrocatalytic performances were modulated by changing the mass ratio of the $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ precursors. The series of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF presented 2D graphene-like nanosheets and amorphous structures and provided more active sites and defects than sole-metal MOFs. Moreover, the results of XRD and XPS indicate that the as-prepared $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOFs comprised multiple metal valence states $(\mathrm{Co}^{0}/\mathrm{Co}^{2+}/\mathrm{Co}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}/N i^{3+}}$ ). The catalytic mechanism for MOR is proposed to be the conversion between different valence states of Co or Ni. Among the different kinds of $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF, the $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF-2 catalyst demonstrated outstanding MOR performance, giving a current density of $92.8\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $1.6\mathrm{V}\nu\mathrm{s}$ . RHE in 1.0 M KOH solution containing $0.5{\bf M}$ methanol. Further, it showed low overpotentials of $119\mathrm{mV}$ and 1.40 V vs. RHE at the current density of $10\mathrm{mAcm^{-1}}$ toward HER and OER, respectively. Furthermore, it displayed superior catalytic capability for water splitting and long-term durability and stability. Compared with conventional MOF-based electrocatalysts, the proposed $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{3-x}(H A B)_{2}}$ MOF catalyst demonstrated several advantages, including (i) increased competitiveness due to its conductivity; (ii) structure retention due to its feasible synthesis without requiring calcination at high temperatures or other further treatment; and (iii) excellent electrocatalytic performances due to the strong synergistic effect between Co and Ni. Overall, this work provides a possible approach to bring MOFs into the range of conductivity values, holding great potential in the application of the MOR and overall water splitting. Moreover, we do believe that this work may drive more researchers to exploit more semiconductive or conductive catalysts toward energy conversion and storage and to get more precise insights into the mechanism about the synergistic effect of different metal ions.
